/*
==========================================================
HEAP / PRIORITY QUEUE REVISION NOTES
==========================================================

1. Java PriorityQueue = MIN HEAP by default

    PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();

    Root always contains the SMALLEST element.

----------------------------------------------------------

2. Max Heap

    PriorityQueue<Integer> pq =
        new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());

    Root always contains the LARGEST element.

----------------------------------------------------------

3. Common Operations

    pq.offer(x);      // insert
    pq.poll();        // remove root
    pq.peek();        // read root
    pq.size();        // current size
    pq.isEmpty();     // empty check

----------------------------------------------------------

4. Time Complexity

    offer()  -> O(log n)
    poll()   -> O(log n)
    peek()   -> O(1)

----------------------------------------------------------

5. Interview Rules

    Top K Largest     -> Min Heap of size K
    Kth Largest       -> Min Heap of size K

    Top K Smallest    -> Max Heap of size K
    Kth Smallest      -> Max Heap of size K

    Top K Frequent    -> HashMap + Min Heap

    Merge K Lists     -> Min Heap

    Dijkstra          -> Min Heap

    Median Stream     -> Two Heaps

==========================================================
TOP K LARGEST TEMPLATE
==========================================================
*/

class Solution {

    public int[] topKLargest(int[] nums, int k) {

        // Min Heap
        // Why Min Heap?
        // Because we want quick access to the SMALLEST element
        // among our current Top K elements.
        PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>();


        // Process every number
        for (int num : nums) {

            // Add current number
            minHeap.offer(num);

            /*
             Why check size > k ?

             We only want to keep K best elements.

             If heap grows beyond K,
             remove the weakest candidate.

             Since this is a Min Heap,
             root = smallest element.

             poll() removes the smallest element.
            */
            if (minHeap.size() > k) {
                minHeap.poll();
            }
        }


        /*
         After processing all numbers,

         heap contains only Top K largest elements.

         Example:

         [7,2,15,8,20,11]
         k = 3

         Heap finally contains:

         [11,15,20]
        */


        int[] result = new int[k];

        /*
         Heap gives values from smallest to largest.

         Poll order:

         11
         15
         20

         To return:

         [20,15,11]

         fill array from right to left.
        */
        for (int i = k - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            result[i] = minHeap.poll();
        }

        return result;
    }
}

/*
==========================================================
HEAP COMPARATORS (VERY IMPORTANT)
==========================================================

By default:

PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();

Java creates MIN HEAP.

Smallest element stays at top.

----------------------------------------------------------
CUSTOM COMPARATOR SYNTAX
----------------------------------------------------------

(a, b) -> ?

Think:

a = first element
b = second element

Comparator decides:

negative  -> a comes first
positive  -> b comes first
zero      -> same

----------------------------------------------------------
MIN HEAP
----------------------------------------------------------

PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap =
    new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> a - b);

OR

PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap =
    new PriorityQueue<>();

Both are same.

Example:

5 2 8 1

Top:

1

----------------------------------------------------------
MAX HEAP
----------------------------------------------------------

PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap =
    new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> b - a);

Example:

5 2 8 1

Top:

8

Why?

For (8,2)

b-a

2-8=-6

negative

8 goes ahead of 2

Larger elements move upward.

----------------------------------------------------------
ANOTHER WAY
----------------------------------------------------------

PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap =
    new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());

Same thing.

----------------------------------------------------------
TOP K FREQUENT ELEMENTS (LC 347)
----------------------------------------------------------

HashMap<Integer,Integer> freq

1 -> 3
2 -> 2
3 -> 1

Store NUMBERS in heap.

PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap =
    new PriorityQueue<>(
        (a,b) -> freq.get(a)-freq.get(b)
    );

Why?

Compare frequencies instead of values.

----------------------------------------------------------

For:

a=2
b=1

freq(2)=2
freq(1)=3

2-3=-1

negative

2 comes first.

Smaller frequency stays at top.

Perfect for Top K Frequent.

----------------------------------------------------------
PAIR COMPARISON
----------------------------------------------------------

PriorityQueue<int[]> pq =
    new PriorityQueue<>(
        (a,b) -> a[1]-b[1]
    );

Assume:

[value,freq]

[1,3]
[2,2]
[3,1]

Comparing:

a[1]
b[1]

means:

compare frequencies.

Heap is ordered by frequency.

----------------------------------------------------------
K CLOSEST POINTS
----------------------------------------------------------

[x,y,distance]

PriorityQueue<int[]> pq =
    new PriorityQueue<>(
        (a,b) -> b[2]-a[2]
    );

Why reverse?

Need MAX HEAP.

Largest distance on top.

Easy to remove farthest point.

----------------------------------------------------------
STRING COMPARATOR
----------------------------------------------------------

PriorityQueue<String> pq =
    new PriorityQueue<>(
        (a,b) -> a.length()-b.length()
    );

Smaller length first.

----------------------------------------------------------
LC 692 TOP K FREQUENT WORDS
----------------------------------------------------------

PriorityQueue<String> pq =
    new PriorityQueue<>(
        (a,b) -> {

            if(freq.get(a)==freq.get(b))
                return b.compareTo(a);

            return freq.get(a)-freq.get(b);
        }
    );

First compare frequency.

If same frequency:

compare alphabetically.

----------------------------------------------------------
MOST IMPORTANT THING
----------------------------------------------------------

(a,b)->a-b

Smaller value rises up.

MIN HEAP

----------------------------------------------------------

(a,b)->b-a

Larger value rises up.

MAX HEAP

----------------------------------------------------------

(a,b)->map.get(a)-map.get(b)

Compare using frequency.

----------------------------------------------------------

(a,b)->a[1]-b[1]

Compare using second column.

----------------------------------------------------------

(a,b)->a[0]-b[0]

Compare using first column.

==========================================================
INTERVIEW SHORTCUT
==========================================================

a-b      -> MIN HEAP

b-a      -> MAX HEAP

freq(a)-freq(b)
         -> frequency heap

a[1]-b[1]
         -> sort by second value

a[0]-b[0]
         -> sort by first value
==========================================================
*/
